# Project: FlyRank Capstone

## Stack
- Frontend: React + Next.js
- Backend/API: Node.js
- Package manager: npm

## Conventions
- Use functional components with hooks (no class components)
- Use TypeScript where possible
- Follow Conventional Commits for all commit messages (feat:, fix:, docs:, chore:, etc.)
- Keep components small and single-purpose
- Use ESLint + Prettier formatting

## AI Assistant Notes
- Prioritize clean, readable code over cleverness
- Explain non-obvious logic with comments
- Ask before introducing new dependencies

## Rules learned from FE-02 workflow drill
- Any auth or login logic must be checked against actual registered data, never 
  assume success. If credentials are not matched against a real or mock data 
  source, treat the form as visually complete but functionally incorrect.
- All form submit handlers must call event.preventDefault() in onSubmit, and any 
  non-submit button inside a <form> (e.g. show/hide password) must explicitly use 
  type="button" to avoid accidentally triggering submission.
- A feature is not done when automated tests pass; it is done when it has also 
  been manually verified in the browser on localhost. Test suites can pass while 
  real browser behavior still differs (e.g. environment-specific issues like 
  network vs localhost).